 a certain year had a mean of 507 and standard deviation of 111, and follow a normal distribution.","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"w3XXX4KQPI"}],"key":"eK990W7gLu"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"One of the criteria for admission to a certain engineering school is an SAT-M score in the top 2% of scores. How does this translate to an actual SAT-M score? In other words, what is the 98th percentile of the SAT-M distribution?","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"key":"uaZoVbbXG7"}],"key":"j1ZJH8Qyfp"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-68378715a95f021281d81f443f976cac.mjs","model":{"question":"What z-value corresponds to the top 2% (i.e., a probability of 0.98 below)?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"z ≈ +2.05","feedback":"Correct! z ≈ +2.05 leaves about 2% of the area above it."},{"correct":false,"text":"z ≈ −2.05","feedback":"z = −2.05 marks the BOTTOM 2%, not the top."},{"correct":false,"text":"z = 0.98","feedback":"0.98 is the probability, not the z-value."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Look for 0.9800 inside the table (the closest value is 0.9798).","explanation":""},"key":"hoI7hpTfzH"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-68378715a95f021281d81f443f976cac.mjs","model":{"question":"So how high must a student score on the SAT-M to be in the top 2%?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"About 735","feedback":"Correct! x = 507 + 2.05(111) ≈ 735. A score of about 735 or higher puts a student in the top 2%."},{"correct":false,"text":"About 619","feedback":"619 corresponds to z ≈ 1, which is only about the 84th percentile."},{"correct":false,"text":"About 509","feedback":"Remember to multiply z by the standard deviation before adding the mean."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Unstandardize: x = 507 + 2.05(111).","explanation":""},"key":"bcxteCPnYk"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comment","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"key":"bu2ujumB1E"}],"key":"xYSRoGzqG8"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":105,"column":1},"end":{"line":105,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the days before computer software could easily provide high degrees of precision, limitations in the precision of the normal table were offset by the process of interpolating. Such calculations are now considered obsolete. For most purposes, it is fine simply to make do with the closest table value provided. And remember: statistical software or a calculator can find any normal probability or percentile directly, without a table—follow your instructor’s technology instructions.","position":{"start":{"line":105,"column":1},"end":{"line":105,"column":1}},"key":"LVl8INmNni"}],"key":"WVl8XfMvwr"}],"class":"important","key":"y8FviuGU8h"}],"key":"WTzsA4kZbD"}],"key":"FfOGI0Civo"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Normal Applications: From Values to Probabilities","url":"/pages/m11-applications-1-of-2","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"},"next":{"title":"Why Approximate the Binomial with a Normal?","url":"/pages/m11-normal-approximation-to-the-binomial-1-of-3","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}